This phase focuses on capturing images, processing them on the client-side, and then using an external API for AI-based organism identification. The mobile camera trigger initiates image capture, followed by client-side image manipulation including EXIF/GPS stripping, resizing, and thumbnail generation. Finally, the image is sent to an endpoint that leverages a Google AI model for taxonomic classification.


Focus is now on integrating the AI-driven organism identification within the user interface, incorporating verification mechanisms and fact retrieval. Server-side validation of taxonomic data will be implemented using external resources, flagging matches as verified. User interface components are being designed for candidate selection and confirmation, with fact cards generated from extracted information and detail views for each entry.
Camera capture UX presents a critical decision between using native system camera capabilities or a custom in-browser implementation. The user interface flow needs to balance instant image preview and retake actions against background upload and optimistic map pin placement.


A multi-select question tool is needed to gather user preferences on UI presentation of AI candidates, handling of low-confidence or non-organism results, fact card display, verification badge rendering, walk integration, and offline behavior. This approach ensures user input informs design decisions. The tool's definition and option format require review to ensure effective user response capture.
